### Architectural Introduction
The expressive power of MongoDB's query and mutation engine comes from its dollar-prefixed (`$`) operator vocabulary. Rather than parsing text-based SQL strings, MongoDB embeds **Query Operators** inside filter documents to select records and **Update Operators** inside modification documents to mutate scalar and array fields in-place. Mastering all 13 operators across comparison, logical, scalar update, and array update categories is essential for full-stack NoSQL engineering.

### Core Architectural Analysis
1. **Comparison Operators (`$gt`, `$gte`, `$lt`, `$lte`, `$ne`)**: Nested inside a field's filter value, these operators test whether a value is strictly greater (`$gt`), greater or equal (`$gte`), less (`$lt`), less or equal (`$lte`), or not equal (`$ne`) to a target threshold (e.g., `{ age: { $gte: 18 } }` or `{ age: { $gt: 16, $lt: 10 } }`).
2. **Set Membership Operators (`$in`, `$nin`)**: `$in` selects documents whose field matches any element in a target array (`{ age: { $in: [17, 18, 19] } }`), while `$nin` selects values absent from the array.
3. **Logical Operators (`$or`, `$and`)**: Placed at the top level with an array of condition objects, `$or` matches when any sub-condition holds (`{ $or: [{ age: { $gte: 19 } }, { name: "Rahul" }] }`), whereas `$and` requires all sub-conditions to hold.
4. **Scalar Update Operators (`$set`, `$inc`)**: Inside `updateOne()` or `updateMany()`, `$set` updates a field's value (`{ $set: { age: 20 } }`), while `$inc` atomically increments a numeric field (`{ $inc: { attempts: 1 } }`).
5. **Array Update Operators (`$push`, `$pull`)**: `$push` appends a value to an array (`{ $push: { subjects: "History" } }`), whereas `$pull` removes matching values from an array (`{ $pull: { subjects: "Science" } }`).
6. **Filter + Update Synergy**: Any query operator can filter documents in the first argument of `updateOne()`/`updateMany()` while update operators mutate fields in the second argument.

### Complete 13-Operator Master Taxonomy Table
| Operator Family | Operator Tokens | Purpose & Placement | Canonical Syntax Example |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Comparison** | `$gt`, `$gte`, `$lt`, `$lte`, `$ne` | Threshold comparison inside field | `db.students.find({ age: { $gte: 18 } })` |
| **Array Query** | `$in`, `$nin` | Match / exclude values in array | `db.students.find({ age: { $in: [17, 18, 19] } })` |
| **Logical Query** | `$or`, `$and` | Combine array of condition objects | `db.students.find({ $or: [{ age: { $gte: 19 } }, { name: "Rahul" }] })` |
| **Scalar Update** | `$set`, `$inc` | Set field value / increment number | `{ $set: { age: 20 }, $inc: { attempts: 1 } }` |
| **Array Update** | `$push`, `$pull` | Add to array / remove from array | `{ $push: { subjects: "History" } }` / `{ $pull: { subjects: "Science" } }` |

### Complete Operator Demonstration Script (Slides 14–16)
```javascript
use schoolDB

// 1. Query Operators in Action (Slide 15)
db.students.find({ age: { $gt: 16, $lt: 10 } });
db.students.find({ age: { $in: [17, 18, 19] } });
db.students.find({
  $or: [{ age: { $gte: 19 } }, { name: "Rahul" }]
});

// 2. Update Operators in Action (Slide 16)
db.students.updateOne(
  { name: "Rahul" },
  {
    $set: { age: 20 },
    $inc: { attempts: 1 },
    $push: { subjects: "History" }
  }
);

db.students.updateOne(
  { name: "Rahul" },
  { $pull: { subjects: "Science" } }
);
```

### Architectural Conclusion
Together, MongoDB's comparison, array membership, logical, and atomic update operators provide a complete declarative language for filtering complex document sets and mutating scalar and array fields in-place.

