### Architectural Introduction
In an academic records management system, student profiles undergo continuous state transitions: individual students retake exams, change ages or grades, add electives, or drop courses, while entire cohorts are promoted at the end of an academic year. MongoDB handles both individual and cohort-wide mutations using `db.<collectionName>.updateOne(<filter>, <update>)` and `db.<collectionName>.updateMany(<filter>, <update>)` powered by atomic in-place update operators.

### Core Architectural Analysis
1. **Preservation of Unreferenced Fields**: When `db.students.updateOne({ name: "Rahul" }, { $set: { age: 20 } })` is executed, the `$set` operator modifies strictly the `age` attribute in-place. All other fields on Rahul's document—such as `_id`, `name`, `grade`, `attempts`, and `subjects`—remain completely untouched on disk.
2. **Multi-Operator Atomic Single-Record Mutation**: A single `updateOne()` call can combine `$set` (scalar replacement), `$inc` (numeric counter addition), and `$push` (array element append) inside one `<update>` document: `{ $set: { age: 20 }, $inc: { attempts: 1 }, $push: { subjects: "History" } }`. MongoDB applies all three modifications atomically to the first document matching `{ name: "Rahul" }`.
3. **Targeted Array Element Pruning (`$pull`)**: When Rahul drops a subject, executing `db.students.updateOne({ name: "Rahul" }, { $pull: { subjects: "Science" } })` locates `"Science"` inside the `subjects` array and removes it in-place without affecting `"Math"` or `"History"`.
4. **Cohort-Wide Bulk Promotion (`updateMany`)**: At year-end, executing `db.students.updateMany({ age: 18 }, { $set: { promoted: true } })` (or `{ grade: 8 }, { $set: { promoted: true } }`) scans the entire `students` collection and sets `promoted: true` on every matching student record.
5. **Dynamic Attribute Injection**: If the `promoted` field did not previously exist on the matched student documents, `$set` automatically creates `promoted: true` on each matched document.
6. **Deterministic Scope Control**: Choosing `updateOne()` guarantees at most one document is altered, whereas `updateMany()` updates every matching document.

### Academic State Transition Matrix
| Academic Event | MongoDB Method | Filter Argument | Update Operator Payload | Documents Mutated |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Update Rahul's grade to 10** | `updateOne()` | `{ name: "Rahul" }` | `{ $set: { grade: 10 } }` | First match (`1`) |
| **Update Rahul's age, attempts & add History** | `updateOne()` | `{ name: "Rahul" }` | `{ $set: { age: 20 }, $inc: { attempts: 1 }, $push: { subjects: "History" } }` | First match (`1`) |
| **Remove Science from Rahul's subjects** | `updateOne()` | `{ name: "Rahul" }` | `{ $pull: { subjects: "Science" } }` | First match (`1`) |
| **Promote all students aged 18** | `updateMany()` | `{ age: 18 }` | `{ $set: { promoted: true } }` | All matching ($N$) |
| **Promote all students in grade 8** | `updateMany()` | `{ grade: 8 }` | `{ $set: { promoted: true } }` | All matching ($N$) |

### Complete Academic Update Workflow
```javascript
