// 1. Single-field update on one student (Slide 9 & Doc Section 3)
db.students.updateOne({ name: "Rahul" }, { $set: { grade: 10 } });

// 2. Multi-operator atomic update on one student (Slide 16)
db.students.updateOne(
  { name: "Rahul" },
  {
    $set: { age: 20 },
    $inc: { attempts: 1 },
    $push: { subjects: "History" }
  }
);

// 3. Array element removal using $pull (Slide 16)
db.students.updateOne(
  { name: "Rahul" },
  { $pull: { subjects: "Science" } }
);

// 4. Bulk cohort promotion using updateMany (Slide 9 & Doc Section 3)
db.students.updateMany(
  { age: 18 },
  { $set: { promoted: true } }
);
```

### Architectural Conclusion
By combining `updateOne()` and `updateMany()` with `$set`, `$inc`, `$push`, and `$pull`, MongoDB delivers atomic, non-destructive state transitions across both individual records and bulk student cohorts.

